{"sew":{"id":"955baea1-0147-49e9-8e04-6cf93c5e3b63","word":"sew","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"(transitive) use a needle","word":"costurar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"(transitive) use a needle","word":"coser"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"(intransitive) use a needle","word":"costurar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"(intransitive) use a needle","word":"coser"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Balls were first made of grass or leaves held together by strings, and later of pieces of animal skin sewn together and stuffed with feathers or hay.","bold_text_offsets":[[102,106]],"type":"example","id":"f8b35a58-133f-4254-9a38-9969f753acda"},{"text":"She [Kate Spade] took the label, which originally had been on the inside of the bag, and sewed it to the outside.","ref":"2018 June 5, Jonah Engel Bromwich, Vanessa Friedman, Matthew Schneier, “Kate Spade, whose handbags carried women into adulthood, is dead at 55”, in The New York Times, New York, N.Y.: The New York Times Company, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 06 Jun 2018:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[89,94]],"id":"a4bbe4d4-71e5-4d55-b6b2-3b79e6d7f62d"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["needle","needle"],["thread","thread"],["fabric","fabric"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To use a needle to pass thread repeatedly through (pieces of fabric) in order to join them together."],"glosses":["To use a needle to pass thread repeatedly through (pieces of fabric) in order to join them together."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"b30e9233-e92e-4834-9354-8c580562c673"},{"categories":["English intransitive verbs"],"links":[["needle","needle"],["thread","thread"],["fabric","fabric"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) To use a needle to pass thread repeatedly through pieces of fabric in order to join them together."],"glosses":["To use a needle to pass thread repeatedly through pieces of fabric in order to join them together."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"d168d77f-cc47-4f59-b9b4-112e7e7d809f"},{"examples":[{"text":"to sew money into a bag","bold_text_offsets":[[3,6]],"type":"example","id":"090c90f9-a1fc-43b1-a393-77490a66e548"}],"categories":["English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["into","into#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) Followed by into: to enclose by sewing."],"glosses":["Followed by into: to enclose by sewing."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"7806dec1-6e34-426a-9176-a350b5b41730"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"word":"stitch"}]},"sewer":{"id":"f5349812-c617-4215-8d7e-4a98e911f27c","word":"sewer","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"person who sews clothing","tags":["masculine"],"word":"costureiro"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Up under the roof three men are making boys’ jackets at twenty cents a piece, of which the sewer takes eight, the ironer three, the finisher five cents, and the buttonhole-maker two and a quarter, leaving a cent and three-quarters to pay for the drumming up, the fetching and bringing back of the goods.","ref":"1890, Jacob A[ugust] Riis, “The Sweaters of Jewtown”, in How the Other Half Lives: Studies among the Tenements of New York, New York, N.Y.: Charles Scribner’s Sons, →OCLC, pages 131–132:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[91,96]],"id":"2fc8fef2-819f-477e-a77c-93999b267a29"}],"links":[["sew","sew"]],"glosses":["One who sews."],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"id":"963e0018-8d50-4836-a842-182fc3f40c48"},{"examples":[{"text":"the apple-leaf sewer, Ancylis nubeculana","bold_text_offsets":[[15,20]],"type":"example","id":"89564220-d94e-44d4-9cf7-110f503cf5ae"}],"links":[["tortricid","tortricid"],["moth","moth"],["larva","larva"],["silk","silk"]],"glosses":["A small tortricid moth, the larva of which